// Lesson 4.3 — Chase: The Aggro Radius
// SOLO BUILD (scaffold level 4) — the second rung-4 lesson in the course, and
// like 3.2 it hands over no code and no API table. Every part of this feature
// has been built once already, somewhere else: the radius is 3.1's distance
// comparison, closing on the player is 4.1's movement-toward-a-target, and
// facing the way it walks is 4.2's Direction value. The one genuinely new
// part — and the part the lesson tells the student to slow down on — is
// deriving a direction from two positions that are BOTH moving, rather than
// from input or from a target the student placed.
// The design problem on top of the maths is still the PATROL -> CHASE
// TRANSITION, and the bug that comes from letting both behaviours write the
// same transform. The three coexistence designs stay in the body because the
// socratic and the specification both ask the student to choose between them
// rather than to invent them from nothing.
//
// GROUND TRUTH: EnemyController.cs ships as an empty stub — by now it holds
// the patrol the student wrote in 4.1, and everything in this lesson is an
// addition to that file. The MobileEnemy prefab exists FOUR times in the
// Overworld, and carries no Collider2D and no Rigidbody2D unless 4.1 added them.
//
// STRUCTURE (the editor/code pass, then the "teach it once" cull): an
// "In the Editor" list of the setup and the before-state, then an "In the Code"
// half that gives the one architectural constraint the lesson hands over, the
// three coexistence designs, one compact specification and the socratic. It no
// longer lists the seven parts: at level 4 the decompose prompts the student to
// find those themselves, and handing them over in prose was doing the level-4
// exercise for them.
//
// CULLED for repetition by the "teach it once" pass: the seven-item "from the
// player's seat" acceptance list (its middle items were the third telling of
// the coexistence table's "What the player sees" column, and its last two were
// already in the specification); the six-item reread list 3.2 also dropped (its
// lessons are linked from the decompose block, the steps and the resources);
// two specification bullets that restated the hand-over callout and the
// coexistence table; the subtraction-order and normalizing line from the
// checkpoint for the same reason; the "stand where the enemy ought to notice
// you" editor step (a question, and the checkpoint already carries the radius
// decision); and the enemy-state-machines resource link (a second telling of
// the unit hub's link).
//
// The parts below are NOT rendered at level 4 (see src/lib/decompose.ts) —
// they are written out because they are the record of the intended split, and
// because they are what the lesson hands over verbatim if it is ever taught at
// a lower rung. Do not delete them as "unused".
// SECOND PASS: the decompose turn and socratic q2 were the same question asked
// in two sections — check 13 measured them at 53% overlap, both asking which
// behaviour wins while the enemy is inside its beat AND inside the radius. The
// socratic keeps it, because the coexistence table it argues about is beside it;
// the turn now asks the thing this lesson actually hands over, the value that
// has to be recomputed every frame and what the enemy's speed depends on. Table
// rows and editor steps are back to the 2.x multi-line shape.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'An enemy that comes for me when I get close. That is the whole feature, and like every ' +
    'feature in this course it is not one problem — it is several standing in a line, one of ' +
    'which you have never solved before.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Find the player once, and hold on to them',
      does:
        'Get a reference to the player at startup and keep it, with a guard for the case where there is no player in the scene at all.',
      doc:
        'Your targeting choice from 3.1 — GameObject.FindWithTag(String tag) or a serialized reference — and the null guard that has to follow either one.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Measure how far away they are',
      does: 'Ask for the straight-line distance between the enemy and the player, every frame.',
      doc:
        'Vector3.Distance(a, b) against sqrMagnitude — the same comparison you chose between for the turret in 3.1.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide that close enough means chase',
      does:
        'Compare that distance against a designer-tunable aggro radius and set the enemy\'s mode from the answer.',
      doc:
        'The range condition, and the boundary you argued about at the exact radius, in 3.1. The tuning knob itself is the serialized field you have used since 1.4.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Derive a direction from the two positions',
      does:
        'Subtract the enemy\'s position from the player\'s, in that order, and give the result length one — every frame, because both ends are moving.',
      doc:
        'The genuinely new part. The subtraction and the normalizing are the pair you aimed a projectile with in 3.2; what is new is doing it continuously, at a target you do not control.',
      href: '3.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Move the enemy along that direction',
      does: 'Multiply the direction by a speed and move the transform, frame-rate independently.',
      doc:
        'The movement method you chose for the patrol in 4.1 — the same call, pointed at a target that moves.',
      href: '4.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Face the way it is chasing',
      does:
        'Set the animator\'s Direction value from the direction of travel, so the sprite reads as a hunter instead of a guard.',
      doc:
        'The enemy animator you drove in 4.2 — the four Direction values, and the controller that has no idle state to fall back on.',
      href: '4.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Stop walking the beat while the chase runs',
      does:
        'Guarantee that exactly one behaviour writes the enemy\'s position in any given frame, and decide what the beat remembers when the chase ends.',
      doc:
        'The patrol you wrote in 4.1 — its direction field and the anchor it turns around.',
      href: '4.1.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Most of the parts inside this feature are things you have already built somewhere else: a ' +
    'distance check written for a turret, a movement toward a target written for a guard, an ' +
    'animator value driven from a direction, a tuning knob, a null guard. Only one part has no ' +
    'earlier version, and it is the one to slow down on — a direction worked out from two ' +
    'positions, one of which you do not control, recomputed every frame. That is what a ' +
    'well-sequenced course feels like from the inside: the same parts, standing in a new order, ' +
    'with one genuinely new one among them.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the enemy chase me" cannot be looked up. "Subtract two positions to get a ' +
    'direction" can. If a part is still too big to search for, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'Write the rule down before you code it: name the value this lesson recomputes every frame, why computing it once is not enough, and what the enemy\'s speed depends on without length one.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>4.3 — Chase: The Aggro Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Patrol is calm. One distance check turns a guard into a hunter — and you wrote that check in
    <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>. What is new is not the maths: the target <em>moves</em>.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make an enemy chase me?', 4)}

${renderDecompose('4.3', DECOMPOSE, 4)}

<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> save the Overworld before you touch anything, then
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> the four <code>MobileEnemy</code> instances, then
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy</code>. All four
            share one prefab, so one serialized aggro field is one number for all four. Decide
            whether that is what you want <em>before</em> you write the field.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the prefab root: the <code>EnemyController</code> from 4.1,
            with the collider and body you gave it. A chase needs a body a wall can stop, and 4.1
            already made that true.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the child named <em>Sprite</em>, then <strong>Window →
            Animation → Animator</strong>. Re-read the four <code>Direction</code> values from 4.2:
            a hunter that keeps the guard's facing reads as a bug, and those four integers are how
            you fix it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play and walk near an enemy with the Console open. It
            ignores you and walks its beat. That is the "before" you are about to change.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>No API table: every call today is one you have already made. One file — <code>Assets/Scripts/EnemyController.cs</code>, your patrol from <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Slow down on one part: working out the direction.</strong> Every direction you have written so far came from something you were handed — keys you pressed in <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a>, a target you placed in <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>, a muzzle you aimed at the instant of firing in <a href="3.2.html">3.2</a>. This time both ends move, and it is worked out again from the two positions, every frame. That is where the bugs will be.
        <p>The one fact I will hand you: the vector from the enemy to the player is the player's position minus the enemy's, <em>in that order</em>. Swap the operands and you have built something that runs away instead. Give it length one before you multiply it by a speed — otherwise the enemy's speed depends on how far away you are, and it crawls the moment it reaches you.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The second decision is not about maths. Your <code>Update()</code> patrols unconditionally and now has to chase as well — two behaviours that want the same object, with no earlier lesson to copy from. Pick one of these three on purpose:</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>How the two behaviours coexist</th><th>What the code does each frame</th><th>What the player sees</th><th>The risk</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; patrol is the <code>else</code></strong></td>
            <td>Exactly one of the two runs.</td>
            <td>A clean switch: the moment you cross the line, the enemy turns and comes for you.</td>
            <td>Nothing holds the answer to "am I chasing?", so no other part of your code can ask. It also forces you to decide which wins when you are inside the patrol beat <em>and</em> the radius at once.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; the patrol keeps running anyway</strong></td>
            <td>Both write to the enemy's position in the same frame.</td>
            <td>A stutter, or an enemy that drifts toward you while still dutifully flipping at the end of its beat.</td>
            <td>Two systems moving one object, and their sum is a third movement <em>nobody designed</em>. With the <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a> leash this is easy to miss: the patrol's flip check still measures from the spawn anchor, so it reverses your chase mid-pursuit.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; the patrol is switched off by a flag</strong></td>
            <td>The flag records the mode, and the patrol checks it.</td>
            <td>Same as the first row, at first.</td>
            <td>One extra field, and one extra thing to keep true — and the moment you have a third behaviour, one more clause in every check. Keep this thought for <a href="4.5.html">4.5</a>.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for the chase.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which of the two targeting options from <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a> did you choose, what does that choice cost across four enemy instances, and what does <code>GameObject.FindWithTag(String tag)</code> hand back when nothing matches? Which lifecycle method holds that lookup so it runs once — and does the guard clause after it stop the whole update, or only the chase half, given what the enemy still has to do with no player in the scene?</li>
            <li>Which serialized float lets a designer say how close you must get, and what do you name it so that a stranger reading the Inspector sees its relationship to the patrol knob beside it?</li>
            <li>Which movement call moves the enemy — the one your patrol uses, or a different one? If they differ, what does the Rigidbody2D have to satisfy for both to work?</li>
            <li>Should the patrol's own memory — its target and its anchor — be reset or preserved when the chase ends? Say what the player sees either way.</li>
            <li>How do you print the current mode to the Console so you can watch the transition happen, and which animator <code>Direction</code> value does the enemy carry while chasing? (Re-read your <a href="4.2.html">4.2</a> notes rather than guessing.)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: States, Transitions and Two Writers</h3>
        <ol>
<li>You gave the turret a distance check in 3.1 and it opened fire. What does the enemy do with that same answer that the turret did not do — and why does that make the enemy harder to build?</li>
<li>Your enemy is inside its patrol beat and inside the aggro radius at the same time. Which behaviour must win? Describe exactly what the player sees if you pick the other one.</li>
<li>If both behaviours move the transform in one frame, what is the sum of "walk right at 2 units per second" and "walk toward the player at 2 units per second"? Why is that sum not a chase — and which direction does the enemy actually travel?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Break the chase into parts, and say in one line what each part does and where you built it before</li>
        <li>Compare <code>Vector3.Distance(a, b)</code> against <code>sqrMagnitude</code>, and say which you chose for the enemy and why</li>
        <li>Compare <code>GameObject.FindWithTag(String tag)</code> against a serialized reference, and account for the four enemy instances</li>
        <li>Explain your patrol-to-chase transition, point at the construct that stops both behaviours running at once, and say why two writers on one transform produce motion nobody designed</li>
        <li>Design an aggro radius that reads as fair next to the turret's detection range, and say which of the two the player should meet first</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — find this lesson again by the question you were asking</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — chase and pursuit in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — normalizing, <code>GameObject.FindWithTag(String tag)</code> and vector maths</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.4.html">4.4 — Attack: Close-Range Contact Damage</a>, where catching you finally has a consequence.</p>
</div>`;
